[h1]Better Commerce Screen UI — Changelog[/h1]

Short form. The full, reasoned version lives in [b]CHANGELOG.md[/b] in the source repository.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]1.13[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Fixed: this mod could wipe other mods' settings.[/b] Its per-game data (settlement priorities and merchant orders) sat in its own entries in browser storage instead of the shared "modSettings" record every other mod uses, and several popular mods erase the whole store the moment they see anything else in there - taking everyone's settings with it, including their own. Everything now lives inside the shared record under this mod's own name, and the old entries are cleaned up on first load. Your own priorities and orders carry across.
[/list]

[h2]1.12[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Fixed: "Reassign All" could leave Resources in the pool with empty slots all over your empire.[/b] Three causes, all in the same path. Emptying the empire came back before the game had finished doing it, so the layout that followed ran against a board that was still changing and stopped early. A Resource the game turned down in ONE settlement was set aside for the whole pass instead of just for that settlement, so it was never offered anywhere else. And a single operation the game would not accept ended the run outright, abandoning everything after it.
[*][b]A Treasure Convoy that sails home by itself now says so.[/b] A short line appears at the top of the screen when one unloads, naming the GDP and Gold it just paid out. Automatic return was silent before this — the only sign of it was the convoy no longer being on the map.
[*][b]Several convoys arriving together are announced once, with the totals[/b], rather than as a stack of lines replacing each other faster than any of them could be read.
[*]The figures are the same ones the Treasure Convoys tab already prints on the card, so the two cannot disagree. Nothing about the return itself changed, and the line only appears when [b]Send convoys home[/b] is on.
[*][b]Fixed: the mod reported itself as version 1.10.[/b]
[/list]

[h2]1.11[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Nothing on screen changes and no setting moves.[/b] The third sweep over what the mod costs, aimed at the part that was always going to be the largest: laying out a whole empire.
[*][b]Laying out an empire no longer re-scores the whole empire for every resource it places.[/b] Each choice is still made against the board the last one left behind — but the answers for every OTHER settlement were being thrown away each time, and now only the settlement a resource landed in is recomputed.
[*][b]The board is carried instead of rebuilt for every resource.[/b] Reading every settlement out of the game before every single placement, to reflect a change in one of them, was the other half of the cost. The whole board is still re-read once a second, so nothing that moves your empire from outside goes unnoticed.
[*][b]Dropping one settlement's answers no longer walks every answer the planner is holding[/b] — it was a comparison against the size of the pool times the size of your empire, once per resource placed.
[*][b]Waiting for the game to accept a resource backs off[/b] instead of asking every four milliseconds until it does.
[*][b]The mod indexes the game's modifier tables for resources only.[/b] Around 12 000 modifiers ship in the game's data; a few hundred belong to resources, and the mod was building and keeping indexes over all of it.
[*][b]Fixed: moving to the next age left the previous age's answers in memory.[/b] The game's resource, unit and modifier tables are the age's own — whether Gold is an Empire resource or a Treasure one changes with it — and every cache the mod builds from them now empties when the age ends.
[*][b]A Treasure Convoy pass no longer asks the game about units it has already ruled out[/b], once per tile of every unit you move.
[*][b]A trade route card is matched to its route once per pass instead of twice.[/b]
[/list]

[h2]1.10[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Nothing on screen changes and no setting moves.[/b] Like 1.9, this is about what the mod costs the game — the second sweep over the same ground, because 1.9 fixed the subscriptions and left the work behind them alone.
[*][b]Fixed: a Treasure Convoy asked the pathfinder about every plot you own.[/b] The same mistake 1.9 fixed for Merchants, in the module nobody looked at afterwards — four to eight hundred full pathfinder queries per convoy, three times a turn, at the moment the turn begins. Most of them were land plots, which a naval unit can never enter, and a search that fails is the expensive one. Automatic return is on by default, so this was running for everybody.
[*][b]Settlement priorities are no longer read from disk tens of thousands of times per layout.[/b] A settlement you never set a priority on was the one answer the mod refused to remember, and the scoring asks once per resource-and-settlement pair for every single resource it places.
[*][b]Three more answers were being worked out again for every pair[/b] — whether a conditional bonus applies here, which settlement a requirement is about, and what a settlement's yields stand at.
[*][b]Emptying settlements no longer waits half a second for messages that are not coming[/b], once per resource and once per settlement.
[*][b]Fixed: a wait for your own resource could be ended by another empire's[/b], letting the next step read a board that had not finished changing.
[*][b]Fixed: a padlock on an imported Resource was never dropped when the Resource left.[/b] The mod was filing its own events as somebody else's, because it read the owner of the plot rather than the settlement the event names.
[*][b]Fixed: the Commerce screen could end up disagreeing with the game after a bulk assignment.[/b] Its own model applies one resource per event and keeps only the latest when several arrive together, so tiles went missing from settlement cards. The mod used to give the display a frame after every single placement to try to keep up — a hundred redraws over a full empire, and it did not fix it. The run now goes at full speed and the whole board is put right once, at the end, cards included.
[*][b]The Commerce screen stopped re-asking the game for answers that never change.[/b] Icons were fetched every time one was drawn; the padlock on every assigned Resource composed its own tooltip every frame; each settlement card rebuilt the eight priority names and their explanations from scratch; the GDP figure re-counted every building in the empire several times a second; and Shift was read from the engine on every mouse movement. All of it is remembered now.
[*][b]The Trade Routes tab stops listening when you leave it[/b], instead of keeping five engine subscriptions for the rest of the session.
[*][b]A trade route card asked the same three questions of your whole army[/b] — a tab of forty cards walked it over a hundred times per redraw. It is one walk now.
[*][b]The Empire and Factory tabs no longer rebuild a static index on every visit[/b], and the layout watcher stops watching the whole HUD once it has what it was waiting for.
[/list]
